    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Chicken run: The Briefing for 1 April 2026</title>
      <link>https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/chicken-run-the-briefing-1-april-2026</link>
      <description>India’s competition watchdog has ordered a probe into poultry business Venky’s following a complaint by an animal welfare group. The agency alleged today that the company, which also owns Blackburn Rovers FC, imposes anticompetitive restrictions on farmers for sales of chicks and eggs. Also in The Briefing, Bulgaria’s enforcer raids IT equipment companies, and LG sees a UK court increase a damages award against the company.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/chicken-run-the-briefing-1-april-2026</guid>
      <enclosure url="https://d2qu5xmcgmzxnb.cloudfront." length="0" type="image/jpeg"/>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Apr 2026 07:45:12 +0000</pubDate>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ABA urges UK government to “abandon” CMA’s share of supply test </title>
      <link>https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/aba-urges-uk-government-abandon-cmas-share-of-supply-test</link>
      <description>Key tests defining the UK Competition and Markets Authority’s jurisdiction to intervene in transactions under the country’s merger control regime should be replaced in favour of more “objective” criteria, the American Bar Association has told the UK’s Department for Business and Trade.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/aba-urges-uk-government-abandon-cmas-share-of-supply-test</guid>
      <enclosure url="https://d2qu5xmcgmzxnb.cloudfront." length="0" type="image/jpeg"/>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Apr 2026 14:38:03 +0000</pubDate>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Apple and Google DMCC fixes risk undermining regime, say UK media companies</title>
      <link>https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/apple-and-google-dmcc-fixes-risk-undermining-regime-say-uk-media-companies</link>
      <description>The UK’s Competition and Markets Authority continues to back voluntary commitments from Apple and Google under the country’s digital markets regime, despite leading media publishers assailing the solutions as “extremely weak” and “ill-defined”.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://globalcompetitionreview.com/article/apple-and-google-dmcc-fixes-risk-undermining-regime-say-uk-media-companies</guid>
      <enclosure url="https://d2qu5xmcgmzxnb.cloudfront." length="0" type="image/jpeg"/>
